<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Harun al-Rashid</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/786">786</a>–<a href="/years/809">809</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Harun al-Rashid was Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> who reigned from 786 to 809. His reign is remembered as a high point of Abbasid prosperity and culture in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and is immortalised in The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-thousand-and-one-nights">Thousand and One Nights</a>. He exchanged embassies with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid.jpg" alt="Harun al-Rashid" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 8/9th-century master(s) · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gold_dinar_of_Harun_al-Rashid,_AH_170-193.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Barmakid family belonged to this mixed elite: they were Persian administrators who served the early caliphs and rose to the height of their power as viziers under <b>Harun al-Rashid</b> until their sudden fall in 803.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 800 the Abbasid caliph <b>Harun al-Rashid</b> appointed Ibrahim I ibn al-Aghlab, the son of a Khurasani Arab commander of the Banu Tamim tribe, hereditary emir of Ifriqiya, in answer to the anarchy that had followed the fall of the Muhallabids in that province.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aghlabid-emirate">Aghlabid emirate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Charlemagne exchanged embassies with the Abbasid caliph <b>Harun al-Rashid</b>, an exchange that brought the elephant Abul-Abbas to Aachen in 802.</blockquote><a href="/empire/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Under <b>Harun al-Rashid</b> the region was governed by Yazid ibn Mazyad al-Shaybani, an Arab commander whose family, the Yazidids or Mazyadids, put down roots there.</blockquote><a href="/empire/shirvanshah-state">Shirvanshah State · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Common protagonists are the Abbasid caliph <b>Harun al-Rashid</b>, his vizier Jafar al-Barmaki and the poet Abu Nuwas, and a character often tells a story within which another is told.</blockquote><a href="/work/one-thousand-and-one-nights">One Thousand and One Nights · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>Within two generations, under <b>Harun al-Rashid</b> (r. 786–809) and al-Ma'mun (r. 813–833), the caliphate presided over the richest and most sophisticated society in western Eurasia.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-collapse-problem">The Collapse Problem</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ray, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in Tus, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.6,51.43&amp;year=797" aria-label="Ray on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5849 878 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Harun al-Rashid"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6299 1148h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6299 1148h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.6,51.43&amp;year=797">What was here in 797 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I have read thy letter, O thou son of an unbelieving mother. Thou shalt not hear, thou shalt behold, my reply.&#8221;</p><footer>Answer to the emperor Nikephoros I, 803 (in Gibbon, Decline and Fall, chapter 52) &#183; tr. Edward Gibbon, 1788 &#183; as Gibbon gives it; the reply was a campaign into Asia Minor &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/735/pg735-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
